WEBVTT

00:01.320 --> 00:03.400
[theme song playing]

00:53.280 --> 00:54.280
[knock on door]

00:54.360 --> 00:57.480
Give me a bottle of whiskey
and three glasses, please.

00:57.560 --> 00:59.120
- Scotch or Irish?
- Irish.

01:02.160 --> 01:04.959
I've decided not to go, to the races.

01:05.040 --> 01:08.200
Not unless you give me
another two pound ten shilling,

01:08.280 --> 01:09.320
toward the dress.

01:09.600 --> 01:11.320
I've already given you three.

01:11.400 --> 01:14.440
How much did you pay
for the suit you'll be wearing?

01:14.520 --> 01:15.840
I don't pay for suits.

01:15.920 --> 01:18.840
My suits are on the house
or the house burns down.

01:20.200 --> 01:23.000
So you want me to go
looking like a flower girl?

01:23.160 --> 01:25.280
What I want makes no difference.

01:29.040 --> 01:31.160
It's not me you're dressing up for.

01:48.920 --> 01:50.000
[bottle pops open]

02:01.280 --> 02:04.360
You'll forgive me, Mr. Shelby,
if I indulge a little.

02:04.880 --> 02:06.200
Please.

02:07.080 --> 02:11.000
It takes a lot for a man from Sparkbrook
to step inside this pub.

02:11.520 --> 02:15.360
Anyone with money and good intentions
is welcome in the Garrison.

02:18.520 --> 02:21.600
Now, you said you had business.

02:25.680 --> 02:27.320
It's delicate, Mr. Shelby.

02:28.480 --> 02:31.280
It's a question of who knows
what about what.

02:32.800 --> 02:35.480
Concerns the factory down the road
at the BSA.

02:37.520 --> 02:41.120
Now, as you might know,
most of the paint shop there is Irish.

02:42.040 --> 02:45.320
Big, old place like that,
rumors get started.

02:45.440 --> 02:47.360
Rumors that there was a robbery.

02:50.240 --> 02:51.880
Robbery of what?

02:51.960 --> 02:56.280
[Ryan] Guns, Mr. Shelby.
A serious amount of guns.

02:57.920 --> 03:00.400
[Thomas] And what business
is that of mine?

03:00.480 --> 03:04.400
When it comes to speculation,
you can't beat a factory night shift.

03:05.000 --> 03:07.760
Some say there was word
from the proofing bay...

03:08.360 --> 03:11.000
That it was the Peaky Blinders
who took them.

03:12.120 --> 03:14.120
Your night shift
must be dreaming.

03:14.200 --> 03:16.360
- Maybe they are.
- Maybe they're not.

03:16.440 --> 03:19.480
What we are trying to say is, Mr. Shelby,

03:19.560 --> 03:23.240
that if you were to hear about
the whereabouts of said items,

03:24.320 --> 03:25.840
we would pay good money.

03:25.920 --> 03:29.360
- You have good money?
- We have collections from the pubs.

03:30.720 --> 03:34.000
- For who do you speak?
- [Ryan] The people of Ireland--

03:34.080 --> 03:36.240
The Irish Republican Army.

03:36.520 --> 03:38.880
- For a fact?
- For a fucking fact.

03:44.320 --> 03:48.320
- You think we're jokers?
- Am I laughing?

03:52.040 --> 03:57.920
♪ Oh father, why are you so sad? ♪

03:58.640 --> 04:02.720
♪ On this bright Easter morning ♪

04:02.800 --> 04:04.360
Maguire, will you shut up?

04:04.440 --> 04:09.960
♪ When Irishmen are proud and glad ♪

04:10.040 --> 04:13.120
♪ Of the land where they were born ♪

04:13.200 --> 04:17.000
[Ryan] Maguire, away and shite,
we're trying to do business here.

04:17.079 --> 04:20.680
♪ Oh son, I see sad memories view ♪

04:21.120 --> 04:25.240
♪ Of far-off distant days ♪

04:26.280 --> 04:30.960
♪ When being just a boy like you ♪

04:32.320 --> 04:38.720
♪ I joined the IRA ♪

04:40.520 --> 04:41.960
Bravo.

04:42.400 --> 04:45.360
♪ Where are the lads
Who stood with me ♪

04:45.440 --> 04:49.160
All right, boys, when I know
who knows what about what,

04:49.240 --> 04:50.600
I'll let you know.

04:50.720 --> 04:55.000
♪ I long to see
The boys of the old IRA ♪

04:58.440 --> 05:01.200
I thought you only
allowed singing on Saturday.

05:01.520 --> 05:03.440
Whiskey is good proofing water.

05:03.520 --> 05:05.720
It tells you who's real
and who isn't.

05:05.800 --> 05:07.640
And what did
my countrymen want?

05:07.720 --> 05:09.120
Oh, they're nobodies.

05:09.560 --> 05:12.040
They drink in the Black Swan
in Sparkbrook.

05:12.120 --> 05:15.000
They're only rebels
because they like the songs.

05:15.080 --> 05:16.880
You have sympathies with them?

05:16.960 --> 05:19.280
I have no sympathies
of any description.

05:19.360 --> 05:23.280
Their accents are so thick, it's a wonder
you could understand them.

05:23.360 --> 05:26.960
- Next time I could translate.
- You'd work for me?

05:27.040 --> 05:28.960
I thought I already was.

05:29.040 --> 05:31.200
So you are coming
to the races?

05:33.080 --> 05:35.040
Two pound ten shillings.

05:39.040 --> 05:40.320
Ten shillings.

05:41.520 --> 05:44.960
Buy something red.
To match his handkerchief.

05:45.120 --> 05:46.560
Whose handkerchief?

06:10.560 --> 06:12.080
Ada, what the hell?

06:12.160 --> 06:15.520
I dared myself to run
through their territory in my dress.

06:15.600 --> 06:18.640
- Are you mad?
- Yeah. Mad as hell with all of them.

06:18.720 --> 06:19.760
I love you.

06:21.760 --> 06:23.040
I love you, too.

06:23.120 --> 06:26.000
The more they try and stop us,
the madder I'll be.

06:26.080 --> 06:29.200
- Come on, princess, the vicar's waiting--
- Wait...

06:29.760 --> 06:30.960
How do I look?

06:36.080 --> 06:37.440
Like an angel.

06:47.120 --> 06:48.320
[knocking on door]

06:49.040 --> 06:50.480
[door opens]

06:53.160 --> 06:54.240
Moss?

06:55.520 --> 06:58.400
What do we know
about the Black Swan?

06:58.520 --> 07:01.800
An operative has
provided intelligence,

07:01.880 --> 07:04.640
that it is a place
where IRA men gather.

07:04.720 --> 07:09.000
It's just a lot of drunken navigators
singing songs, sir.

07:09.080 --> 07:11.320
The IRA wouldn't go near them.

07:11.400 --> 07:14.120
Then why are two of them
looking to buy guns?

07:16.560 --> 07:17.920
Do you have names?

07:19.720 --> 07:22.040
Must she do everything for you?

07:22.160 --> 07:23.280
"She", sir?

07:23.360 --> 07:26.680
One female operative
has proved more useful...

07:28.000 --> 07:31.520
Than any of you
great lumps of men.

07:31.600 --> 07:36.080
We are regular police officers, sir.
Not spies.

07:36.160 --> 07:38.960
We can only act when a crime has
been committed.

07:39.040 --> 07:43.160
Perhaps I should send some men down
to the Black Swan to ask questions.

07:43.240 --> 07:45.560
And scare them all into hiding?

07:45.640 --> 07:47.880
Not the best of tactics, Sergeant.

07:47.960 --> 07:51.800
My tactics come
from my experiences in France.

07:51.880 --> 07:56.080
Most of my great lumps of men
served in France, too, sir.

07:58.360 --> 08:01.920
I serve my country every day.

08:03.440 --> 08:05.040
That will be all, sergeant.

08:54.600 --> 08:55.680
[dog barks]

08:55.760 --> 08:57.480
[Ryan] Away and shite!

08:57.560 --> 08:58.960
[dog continues barking]

09:34.560 --> 09:35.880
Fancy me, do ya?

09:37.640 --> 09:39.840
What are you? Copper?

09:42.920 --> 09:44.240
Proddy bitch.

09:45.320 --> 09:46.760
I've seen you!

09:47.080 --> 09:49.680
I've seen your face,
serving at the Garrison.

09:49.760 --> 09:51.200
Come here.

09:51.280 --> 09:55.400
I am taking you in for interrogation
on behalf of the Irish Free State.

09:55.480 --> 09:56.680
[both grunting]

09:57.920 --> 09:59.280
[gunshot]

10:22.320 --> 10:23.760
All right?

10:25.440 --> 10:27.000
Are you armed?

10:29.000 --> 10:30.200
No.

10:31.960 --> 10:33.520
Then I'll tell you.

10:35.480 --> 10:37.920
Ada and Freddie Thorne
were married today.

10:38.040 --> 10:41.200
They defied your orders,
they haven't left the city.

10:53.640 --> 10:55.080
I'll deal with it.

10:58.320 --> 11:01.680
Thomas. I'll deal with it.

11:02.360 --> 11:03.600
Where are they?

11:03.680 --> 11:07.240
Freddie's comrades have safe houses.
Why do you want to know?

11:07.320 --> 11:10.000
I want to send them flowers.
Why do you think?

11:10.080 --> 11:12.240
Would it be so bad if they stayed?

11:13.640 --> 11:15.880
I promised I'd run
Freddie out of town.

11:18.080 --> 11:19.240
Promised who?

11:30.520 --> 11:33.200
I told the coppers
Freddie wouldn't come back.

11:33.320 --> 11:34.800
It was part of the deal.

11:34.880 --> 11:36.840
What bloody deal?

11:36.960 --> 11:40.280
What happened to family votes?
What happened to meetings?

11:42.800 --> 11:46.280
If you let me deal with Ada and Freddie,
it'll end in peace.

11:50.120 --> 11:52.760
Christ knows you have had
your fill of war.

11:57.880 --> 12:02.680
You get Freddie out of town, Pol.
Or else I'll deal with him myself.

12:07.600 --> 12:08.800
[thunder rumbling]

12:16.080 --> 12:18.280
[vomiting]

12:59.520 --> 13:03.200
[Moss] The victim had been drinking
in the Black Swan pub, sir.

13:03.280 --> 13:05.400
The pub that you had told me about.

13:05.480 --> 13:08.200
And a neighbor said that
she saw a young woman

13:08.280 --> 13:10.800
leaving the street
where the body was found.

13:11.840 --> 13:15.640
A woman, sir. I was wondering
if there was a connection.

13:16.160 --> 13:19.120
A connection with what, Sergeant?

13:19.200 --> 13:22.320
You said that your spy
was a woman, sir.

13:22.760 --> 13:25.400
I wondered if there was now a policy.

13:28.760 --> 13:32.920
Shoot to kill.
Like there was in Belfast.

13:34.880 --> 13:37.200
The Republicans are famously factional.

13:38.360 --> 13:40.400
He was killed by one of his own.

13:42.960 --> 13:44.600
That will be all, Sergeant.

13:46.760 --> 13:50.960
And they are not called spies.
They are called operatives.

13:51.040 --> 13:52.880
Operatives sir, right, sir.

13:56.080 --> 13:57.080
[door opens]

13:57.440 --> 13:59.920
[Freddie] That's how you say things.

14:02.600 --> 14:04.920
What the bloody hell
is she doing here?

14:05.840 --> 14:07.560
Two hundred pounds, Freddie.

14:09.840 --> 14:13.280
- For what?
- For us.

14:13.520 --> 14:15.880
For a honeymoon
that goes on forever.

14:20.280 --> 14:23.280
- Where did it come from?
- Family fund.

14:23.360 --> 14:25.920
Pockets of widows
and desperate men.

14:26.000 --> 14:27.160
See sense, Freddie.

14:27.240 --> 14:29.760
[Polly] Tommy is not
going to let this rest.

14:29.840 --> 14:33.640
Times like these, a communist
in the family is bad for business.

14:33.720 --> 14:35.720
You have to leave the city.

14:35.800 --> 14:38.000
You think I can't handle
Tommy Shelby?

14:38.080 --> 14:39.160
You can't.

14:39.240 --> 14:42.800
I am having trouble these days
and I'm twice the man you are.

14:45.960 --> 14:47.680
I went into town.

14:48.480 --> 14:50.760
The Cunard office.

14:50.920 --> 14:53.480
I booked you both tickets
for the next boat,

14:53.560 --> 14:55.200
from Liverpool to New York.

14:55.280 --> 14:57.280
[Ada] America, Freddie.

14:57.360 --> 15:01.040
They've already had their revolution,
you won't have to bother.

15:07.880 --> 15:10.720
Would you ladies
at least let a man sleep on it?

15:43.440 --> 15:45.040
You had no business.

15:48.840 --> 15:51.440
Observe and report.
That is your remit.

15:51.520 --> 15:54.000
I decided we needed to know
where he lived.

15:54.080 --> 15:57.160
I warned you
not to let your personal history

15:57.240 --> 16:00.720
cloud your judgment.
You went after him because he was IRA.

16:00.880 --> 16:04.320
I followed him because I thought
he might have information.

16:04.400 --> 16:05.760
According to the rules,

16:05.840 --> 16:08.800
I should pull you out
of Garrison Lane immediately.

16:08.880 --> 16:11.680
No. Cheltenham is tomorrow.

16:12.920 --> 16:15.240
You take too much upon yourself.

16:16.840 --> 16:19.240
This is an active military mission.

16:19.320 --> 16:22.280
Your words. I am doing my job.

16:22.800 --> 16:27.680
Grace, the death of a base Fenian
does not concern me.

16:27.960 --> 16:29.840
Your welfare does.

16:31.120 --> 16:33.400
Killing a man affects the heart.

16:35.360 --> 16:37.440
And as for my heart...

16:37.520 --> 16:40.200
I know that because
of our family connections,

16:40.280 --> 16:42.320
you take my progress personally.

16:43.600 --> 16:46.080
But I don't need you to be my father.

16:49.000 --> 16:51.000
I will be thinking of you.

17:07.440 --> 17:09.480
[people chattering]

17:16.440 --> 17:19.160
- Mr. Shelby, sir.
- Mr. Zhang.

17:20.839 --> 17:24.640
That suit is sold, sir.
It is being collected this morning.

17:25.000 --> 17:26.119
I know.

17:31.120 --> 17:33.480
Please, gentlemen.
No fighting in here.

17:33.560 --> 17:35.880
It's all right.
I come as a friend.

17:36.880 --> 17:40.320
You're collecting your suit
for Cheltenham, it's very nice.

17:40.400 --> 17:42.280
How the bloody hell do you know?

17:42.360 --> 17:44.520
I know a lot of things, Mr. Kimber.

17:44.600 --> 17:47.520
And as I told you before,
I know the Lee brothers.

17:47.600 --> 17:51.280
And I know they're going to be
at the Cheltenham races as well.

17:51.360 --> 17:55.440
Yes. They're going to lose a lot of money
on fast women and slow nags.

17:55.520 --> 17:58.120
No. They're planning
on showing up in numbers

17:58.200 --> 18:01.280
and robbing your bookies.
Running chalk and rafflers.

18:01.360 --> 18:03.440
You think I can't handle the Lees?

18:03.520 --> 18:06.520
Just a word of warning from a friend.
That's all.

18:08.680 --> 18:10.640
I'll see you at Cheltenham.

18:10.720 --> 18:13.640
I'll wave at you from my box.

18:13.720 --> 18:17.560
If you're coming to the races,
bring that pretty barmaid of yours.

18:17.640 --> 18:19.040
Already invited.

18:20.480 --> 18:21.840
[crowd cheering]

18:22.800 --> 18:24.600
[man in crowd] Go on, Freddie!

18:24.920 --> 18:26.080
[Freddie] Comrades.

18:26.360 --> 18:31.120
As you know, to even gather
like this is today is illegal.

18:31.200 --> 18:34.720
Chief Inspector Campbell has ordered
that we are not allowed

18:34.800 --> 18:37.560
to assemble in groups
of more than three people.

18:37.640 --> 18:38.640
[crowd chuckling]

18:38.720 --> 18:40.360
So, to comply with the law,

18:40.440 --> 18:43.160
please now clump together
in groups of three.

18:43.240 --> 18:44.840
[all laughing]

18:44.920 --> 18:46.080
[whistle blowing]

18:46.240 --> 18:47.600
You hear that, boys?

18:47.680 --> 18:51.200
The same whistles they used to blow
to send us over the top,

18:51.280 --> 18:54.040
they now blow to try
and break us up.

18:54.120 --> 18:56.160
So before the Specials get here,

18:56.240 --> 18:58.760
raise a hand,
all those who want to strike!

18:58.840 --> 19:00.000
[men cheering]

19:02.160 --> 19:03.720
Now, disperse!

19:03.800 --> 19:06.480
Disperse and take
the message home!

19:06.840 --> 19:09.040
United we will never be defeated!

19:09.120 --> 19:11.120
[all] Yes!

19:19.600 --> 19:21.400
- Tommy!
- Tommy.

19:21.800 --> 19:23.640
What the hell's been happening?

19:23.720 --> 19:26.680
The police have just raided a rally
at the factory.

19:26.760 --> 19:28.960
You think your Freddie Thorne's back?

19:29.040 --> 19:32.080
I know he is and he's with Ada.
How hard can it be

19:32.160 --> 19:35.480
to find the only girl in Birmingham
with four-inch heels?

19:35.560 --> 19:36.920
She's with the commies.

19:37.000 --> 19:39.600
They have little rat holes
all over the city.

19:39.920 --> 19:41.880
I just want to know where she is.

19:41.960 --> 19:44.920
Tommy, you'd best have a word
with Arthur as well.

19:45.000 --> 19:47.360
What's wrong with bloody Arthur?

19:47.800 --> 19:49.800
He's got the Flanders Blues again.

20:29.480 --> 20:33.360
People keep asking me questions
that I don't know the answer to.

20:37.920 --> 20:40.320
"Is it true your Ada got married?"

20:42.440 --> 20:43.880
I say, "I don't know."

20:46.160 --> 20:48.120
"Where is she living now?"

20:50.240 --> 20:51.680
"I don't know."

20:53.640 --> 20:54.960
"Arthur,

20:57.720 --> 21:00.360
who killed the Paddy
from the Black Swan?"

21:02.920 --> 21:05.320
I go, "What Paddy?"

21:09.520 --> 21:11.000
They say,

21:12.440 --> 21:15.480
"Is it you Peaky Blinders,

21:15.560 --> 21:19.080
who stole the guns from the BSA?"

21:28.000 --> 21:29.600
What guns, Tommy?

21:31.040 --> 21:34.520
Arthur, after your beating,
I thought you needed a break--

21:34.600 --> 21:37.240
What bloody guns, Tommy?

21:39.400 --> 21:41.320
Arthur, I was going to tell you.

21:41.400 --> 21:42.960
You were going to tell me.

21:43.080 --> 21:45.400
Arthur, listen to me.

21:47.320 --> 21:51.080
You've had a hard time
these past few years. God knows you have.

21:51.160 --> 21:52.760
You deserve some rest.

21:55.200 --> 22:00.080
We had some luck.
Some bloody luck.

22:00.320 --> 22:03.280
It fell off a wagon into our laps.

22:03.360 --> 22:08.400
And all you need to know is,
it's us that has the machine guns now.

22:08.480 --> 22:10.400
And it's them that's in the mud.

22:12.680 --> 22:14.040
All right?

22:21.760 --> 22:23.120
Come on.

22:24.200 --> 22:27.440
I have a surprise for you. Come on.

22:52.080 --> 22:54.960
A surprise? Where is she?

22:57.320 --> 22:59.760
What is it you've
always wanted, Arthur?

23:02.040 --> 23:04.920
When we were in France
you used to say,

23:05.040 --> 23:08.480
"When I get back to England,
I want to own my own pub".

23:11.320 --> 23:12.600
Well...

23:16.360 --> 23:20.000
Now you've gone soft.
You've gone soft, Tommy.

23:23.040 --> 23:25.280
How do we know it's for sale?

23:25.360 --> 23:27.520
Everything's for sale to us, Arthur.

23:28.880 --> 23:31.520
We're making a lot
of money these days.

23:31.600 --> 23:35.400
We need a legitimate business
to pass the money from the shop.

23:36.560 --> 23:39.080
I wouldn't know what to do.

23:39.160 --> 23:44.160
You spent two-thirds of your life in pubs.
Just pour it instead of drinking it.

23:46.160 --> 23:50.200
- But I can still drink it, right?
- Your pub, you do what you want.

23:53.640 --> 23:57.440
[Harry] Ah! Sorry, gentlemen,
I didn't hear you come in.

23:58.720 --> 23:59.920
What can I get you?

24:19.520 --> 24:23.440
Bloody kids.
The other one's punctured as well.

24:25.760 --> 24:28.400
Mr. Campbell wants an explanation.

24:28.480 --> 24:33.880
Today, some rabble-rouser
Union man brought the BSA out on strike.

24:35.440 --> 24:37.960
It was Freddie Thorne.

24:38.040 --> 24:40.680
I thought you promised
he wouldn't come back.

24:40.760 --> 24:43.480
I know he's in town.
I'm dealing with it.

24:43.560 --> 24:47.120
Mr. Campbell thought that
you controlled your territories.

24:47.520 --> 24:48.920
Yeah.

24:49.880 --> 24:51.600
I said, I'm dealing with it.

24:53.560 --> 24:55.160
I heard that,

24:56.720 --> 24:59.200
Freddie married your sister.

24:59.280 --> 25:03.480
Some family you've got, eh?
I bet you can't wait for Christmas.

25:05.240 --> 25:08.120
Deliver Freddie Thorne to us,

25:08.200 --> 25:11.080
or we'll take your sister in
as an accomplice.

25:11.160 --> 25:13.320
She'll get four years for sedition.

25:15.600 --> 25:19.080
Or you can turn him in.
And your sister goes free.

25:27.240 --> 25:29.320
I'll say good night then, Tom.

25:55.960 --> 25:58.640
- Have you seen Freddie Thorne?
- No.

25:58.720 --> 25:59.960
Or Tommy.

26:14.240 --> 26:16.960
- I have to find Freddie.
- Drink this first.

26:17.040 --> 26:20.880
No, I have to find them,
I think they're going to kill each other.

26:20.960 --> 26:22.840
Wait. Who is going to kill who?

26:24.000 --> 26:25.280
We need to talk.

26:32.320 --> 26:35.000
What exactly is it
that you want, Freddie?

26:36.240 --> 26:37.760
I came to tell you...

26:41.240 --> 26:43.040
That's not gonna work, Tommy.

26:45.400 --> 26:48.360
Polly came round.
She gave me that.

26:51.120 --> 26:54.880
Well, Polly must have had a rush of blood.

26:54.960 --> 26:56.040
Or port wine.

26:56.120 --> 26:57.840
Your honesty is appreciated.

26:57.920 --> 27:00.640
Now, if you're not going
to use that thing...

27:00.720 --> 27:04.160
I'm not finished! Sit down.

27:04.280 --> 27:05.600
Sit down. Sit!

27:11.600 --> 27:13.520
Talk to me about the guns.

27:15.360 --> 27:19.080
Do you remember we used
to jump in here and see who could swim--

27:19.160 --> 27:21.360
I'm here to talk business, Tommy.

27:23.960 --> 27:26.200
Do you reckon we could still do it?

27:40.320 --> 27:44.280
You loaded Ada with your bastard,
because she's a Shelby.

27:45.840 --> 27:49.320
You thought it would mean
you'd be somebody.

27:50.120 --> 27:53.600
I won't let you fuck up
my sister's life for your cause.

27:58.040 --> 27:59.480
My God.

28:04.280 --> 28:06.320
You actually believe that.

28:12.920 --> 28:14.520
I love her, Tommy.

28:16.040 --> 28:19.040
I've loved her since she was nine
and I was 12.

28:20.640 --> 28:22.640
And she loves me the same.

28:30.680 --> 28:32.760
Do you even know the word?

28:36.040 --> 28:38.280
This marriage will not stand.

28:48.520 --> 28:49.560
[shushes]

29:10.040 --> 29:12.920
Freddie didn't want your money.

29:13.920 --> 29:17.400
And now the coppers are saying
if we don't turn Freddie in,

29:17.480 --> 29:20.360
they'll put Ada on the arrest warrant
as well.

29:20.440 --> 29:23.280
That's where your compassion
gets you, Poll.

29:23.440 --> 29:25.920
From now on, we do it my way.

29:27.600 --> 29:28.840
Or what?

30:07.320 --> 30:08.600
Ready?

30:09.600 --> 30:11.680
[Thomas] They are getting closer.

30:15.360 --> 30:17.520
- I'll lead.
- Watch yourself, Danny.

30:17.600 --> 30:18.880
Tommy...

30:26.720 --> 30:28.120
Be careful, Danny.

30:41.360 --> 30:42.520
[men whispering]

30:56.000 --> 30:57.480
[men screaming]

31:00.480 --> 31:01.640
Tommy!

31:02.360 --> 31:03.640
[screaming]

31:14.880 --> 31:16.040
Tommy!

31:16.120 --> 31:17.280
[banging on door]

31:17.360 --> 31:18.640
[Danny] Tommy!

31:19.280 --> 31:20.520
Tommy!

31:21.600 --> 31:22.760
Tommy!

31:30.000 --> 31:31.240
It's open.

31:37.240 --> 31:39.320
Private Whizz-Bang reporting, sir.

31:40.080 --> 31:41.200
At ease.

31:55.360 --> 31:57.600
So, what news from London?

31:58.440 --> 32:00.200
I was in a pub.

32:00.280 --> 32:04.240
It's called the Mother Redcap.
An Irish pub.

32:04.440 --> 32:07.960
I got talking to some old bloke
about Birmingham.

32:08.040 --> 32:11.080
He said there's been trouble.
An IRA man shot.

32:12.720 --> 32:14.960
He said a lot,
but the only bit I heard

32:15.040 --> 32:19.360
was that their high command think
it's the Peaky Blinders who shot him.

32:19.920 --> 32:22.320
I came up on the next boat to warn you.

32:23.640 --> 32:24.880
Is it true?

32:26.160 --> 32:27.160
No.

32:28.440 --> 32:31.120
But lies travel faster than the truth.

32:33.680 --> 32:36.160
Get a message to them.

32:36.240 --> 32:38.560
Tell them to send
someone to parley.

32:38.640 --> 32:42.800
Tell them there's been a misunderstanding
and we don't want any trouble.

32:45.360 --> 32:47.720
You've got enough trouble.
Right, Tommy?

32:49.360 --> 32:52.120
The whiskey and the smoke.

32:57.480 --> 32:59.360
I can smell it in the air.

33:02.520 --> 33:04.520
I use it sometimes myself.

33:05.480 --> 33:07.080
Call it "my sweetheart".

33:10.520 --> 33:12.680
They gave us the worst job, Tommy.

33:13.680 --> 33:14.960
Yeah.

33:16.360 --> 33:18.600
And we fucking volunteered.

33:32.600 --> 33:35.040
Sometimes it lasts all night.

33:37.360 --> 33:41.120
And I lie here
and I listen to the shovels,

33:42.520 --> 33:45.960
and the picks against that wall there.

33:50.640 --> 33:55.400
And I pray the sun will come up
at the curtains before they break through.

33:57.160 --> 33:59.440
No, I don't pray.

33:59.520 --> 34:00.840
I hope.

34:02.800 --> 34:05.360
And sometimes it happens.

34:05.440 --> 34:06.920
The sun beats them.

34:08.520 --> 34:09.760
But mostly...

34:10.719 --> 34:12.159
[faint thudding sound]

34:13.000 --> 34:14.920
The shovels beat the sun.

35:03.440 --> 35:04.720
[sighs]

35:18.360 --> 35:19.600
[crying]

35:36.040 --> 35:37.880
Your sister was here.

35:42.640 --> 35:44.560
She was worried.
You and Freddie.

35:45.400 --> 35:46.720
It had made her sick.

35:48.480 --> 35:51.600
She's all right, but in her condition
she needs peace.

35:53.520 --> 35:56.760
- Women talk.
- That is something they do.

35:58.800 --> 36:00.440
She talked about you.

36:02.720 --> 36:05.080
She said you keep
everything locked up.

36:05.920 --> 36:08.480
Well, that's what men do.

36:09.600 --> 36:11.480
Your sister's nice. I like her.

36:12.200 --> 36:14.080
It can't be easy for her.

36:14.160 --> 36:17.480
Her brother and her husband
fighting over the same thing.

36:22.080 --> 36:23.720
Men should talk, too.

36:25.000 --> 36:26.240
To you?

36:29.000 --> 36:33.080
Why not?
I'm a barmaid. It's my job.

36:33.160 --> 36:35.800
Men always tell their troubles
to a barmaid.

36:37.080 --> 36:39.840
What is it you and Freddie
are fighting over?

36:52.600 --> 36:55.640
I'll meet you here
at nine o'clock tomorrow morning.

36:59.000 --> 37:00.520
Did you buy a dress?

37:01.280 --> 37:03.120
Yes. I bought a dress.

37:04.280 --> 37:05.680
How does it look?

38:23.480 --> 38:26.400
[Charlie] He's changed the oil
and greased her up.

38:26.480 --> 38:29.440
So will it get me all the way
to Cheltenham, Curly?

38:29.520 --> 38:31.800
He's good with motors
but it pains him.

38:31.880 --> 38:34.520
No heart in motorcars.
I can't talk to them.

38:34.600 --> 38:38.160
Yeah. Tommy might need
to make a fast getaway.

38:38.240 --> 38:40.320
The Lees will be
all over the track.

38:40.400 --> 38:42.800
And Kimber's men.
And his coppers.

38:42.880 --> 38:45.160
They control the law
down there, Tommy.

38:45.240 --> 38:47.240
Give her a turn for me, Charlie.

38:53.600 --> 38:55.080
This car only seats four.

38:55.160 --> 38:58.640
You'll need more men than that
if you're to come back alive.

38:58.720 --> 39:00.480
It'll be just me and a girl.

39:08.880 --> 39:11.760
Is it just the two of us
going to the races?

39:11.840 --> 39:13.120
Something like that.

39:48.000 --> 39:49.600
Attention!

39:50.040 --> 39:51.200
Right.

39:51.280 --> 39:53.760
This is what's known
as your final briefing

39:53.840 --> 39:56.240
before going over the top.

39:56.320 --> 39:59.360
Sixty miles down that road
is Cheltenham racetrack.

39:59.440 --> 40:01.960
Johnny, what is
our mission, boy?

40:02.040 --> 40:05.120
To stick it to
the Lee family, Arthur!

40:05.200 --> 40:06.600
That's right.

40:06.680 --> 40:09.640
The Lees are skimming money
off the legal bookies.

40:09.720 --> 40:14.560
Running chalk, selling rafflers,
then beating up them as won't buy.

40:14.640 --> 40:17.360
But today we're
going to stop them.

40:17.440 --> 40:21.000
What about Kimber's men?
I thought he had his own protection.

40:21.080 --> 40:24.160
Kimber has let his troops go rotten.

40:24.240 --> 40:28.280
They're on the take
from the Lees to look the other way.

40:28.360 --> 40:31.160
We're gonna show Kimber
how it should be done.

40:31.240 --> 40:32.960
Now take what you're good at.

40:33.040 --> 40:35.680
Those of you with guns,
keep them out of it

40:35.760 --> 40:39.240
unless a gun is pulled on you.
We want this done quietly.

40:41.520 --> 40:43.920
[John] So when do we
share out the cash?

40:44.000 --> 40:47.160
We don't.
We're not keeping the cash.

40:47.240 --> 40:49.320
You're in Tommy's army now, boys.

40:50.280 --> 40:51.680
Trust only kin.

40:53.480 --> 40:54.680
Let's go.

41:10.160 --> 41:12.440
[announcer speaking indistinctly]

41:14.800 --> 41:16.920
Are you sure
we're allowed in here?

41:17.000 --> 41:19.880
Well, I prefer to come to the races
the back way.

41:19.960 --> 41:24.000
It keeps me out of trouble.
Tracks are lawless places.

41:24.080 --> 41:26.800
I can't stand petty criminals.
This way.

41:26.880 --> 41:28.360
Will we get to lay a bet?

41:28.440 --> 41:31.240
Nah. Gambling is for mugs.
This way.

41:31.680 --> 41:36.120
You're lucky you're with me or you'd be
wasting your money on fixed races.

41:36.200 --> 41:38.560
I always wondered,
how do you fix a race?

41:38.640 --> 41:40.080
How should I know?

41:43.080 --> 41:46.040
- Okay, you do the talking.
- What?

41:46.160 --> 41:49.800
Tell security you are Lady Sarah Duggan--
Excuse us.

41:49.880 --> 41:53.280
Excuse us.
Lady Sarah Duggan of Connemara.

41:53.360 --> 41:57.160
You got lost when you went
to look for the boy riding your horse,

41:57.240 --> 41:59.240
Dandy Flower.
If they ask about me,

41:59.320 --> 42:02.560
say that I'm Prussian and don't speak
a word of English.

42:02.640 --> 42:05.000
Come on, posh girl.
Earn your three quid.

42:10.640 --> 42:12.160
[upbeat music playing]

42:35.600 --> 42:37.360
I still prefer the Garrison.

42:40.960 --> 42:42.320
Do you dance?

42:43.320 --> 42:45.080
If I'm asked properly.

42:47.520 --> 42:51.240
Lady Sarah of Connemara,
will you dance with me?

43:50.200 --> 43:53.400
Roberts, the Peaky Blinders are here.

43:53.480 --> 43:56.640
I told you, Mr. Kimber.
He's got some balls, that one.

43:56.720 --> 43:58.760
And she's got some body, that one.

44:12.000 --> 44:15.600
Chalk for your blackboards.
Five pounds a stick.

44:20.120 --> 44:22.560
It's a pleasure doing business with you.

44:26.360 --> 44:28.800
Hello, Raz. How's business?

44:29.000 --> 44:29.880
[screaming]

44:34.880 --> 44:36.200
Fucker! Get him!

44:42.320 --> 44:43.440
[screaming]

44:46.000 --> 44:49.360
Stay still, Raz,
or I'll take the whole ear off

44:49.440 --> 44:52.080
and you're going to need
your ears to listen.

44:52.160 --> 44:55.720
No more chalking
on Billy Kimber's boys.

44:55.800 --> 44:57.080
Right?

44:58.200 --> 45:00.120
We are the protection now.

45:02.400 --> 45:08.520
I commandeer this stolen money
by order of the Peaky Blinders!

45:35.080 --> 45:37.920
Either your left leg is stronger
than your right,

45:38.000 --> 45:39.800
or we're making a getaway.

45:39.880 --> 45:41.000
Neither.

45:41.080 --> 45:43.480
I hope this doesn't involve
razor blades.

45:43.560 --> 45:45.680
I've decided to move up
in the world.

45:45.760 --> 45:47.680
Become a legitimate businessman.

45:47.760 --> 45:49.640
My gosh. You're serious.

45:49.720 --> 45:51.160
I'm always serious.

45:53.760 --> 45:57.200
We chased the Lees across the track
and down to Devon road.

45:57.280 --> 45:58.720
We got every penny back.

45:59.960 --> 46:03.000
Nice dress.
You can wear that to my pub.

46:03.120 --> 46:05.280
Buy the boys a drink.
Anybody hurt?

46:05.560 --> 46:07.720
A few cuts and bruises.

46:07.800 --> 46:09.480
Off we go, Lady Sarah.

46:16.520 --> 46:17.560
Steady on.

46:18.240 --> 46:19.360
Excuse me.

46:27.200 --> 46:29.000
Your money, Mr. Kimber.

46:30.160 --> 46:32.440
Rescued from the Lee brothers.

46:32.520 --> 46:37.040
And returned to you with
a request for a fair hearing.

46:37.200 --> 46:40.440
Your own protection
is failing, Mr. Kimber.

46:40.520 --> 46:42.520
Your boys are taking cuts.

46:43.480 --> 46:45.720
I want to suggest
that from now on,

46:45.920 --> 46:50.680
you contract out your racetrack security
to the Peaky Blinders.

46:50.760 --> 46:53.680
We would be saving you
a lot of money, Mr. Kimber.

46:53.760 --> 46:55.280
A lot of money.

46:55.360 --> 46:57.360
In return, you give us,

46:58.760 --> 47:00.840
five percent of the take.

47:00.920 --> 47:04.160
And three legal betting pitches
at every race meeting,

47:04.240 --> 47:05.880
north of the River Severn,

47:05.960 --> 47:10.160
rising to six after one year
if we are all satisfied with the service.

47:12.640 --> 47:14.640
What do you say, Mr. Kimber?

47:19.000 --> 47:21.720
I say you talk business
to my accountant.

47:21.800 --> 47:23.440
I want to dance.

47:35.040 --> 47:38.360
Your man said it was all right
for me to have this dance.

48:00.320 --> 48:03.360
How many men can you put
in the field at one time?

48:03.440 --> 48:06.160
There's a lot of men out of work
at the moment.

48:06.240 --> 48:07.920
Two guards for every bookie.

48:08.000 --> 48:09.440
At every meeting?

48:10.960 --> 48:13.960
We have contacts
with good people among the Gypsies.

48:14.040 --> 48:16.840
We'll always know
when the Lees plan to attack.

48:17.800 --> 48:22.240
With all the strikes and troubles,
you can't really depend on the police.

48:22.320 --> 48:24.560
Anyway, we're more honest.

48:25.920 --> 48:28.040
Looks like you two
are making a deal.

48:28.120 --> 48:29.480
We are making progress.

48:31.480 --> 48:34.400
Then let me throw
a small condition into the mix.

48:51.040 --> 48:54.800
So, listen, we're going
to go for dinner at Kimber's house.

48:54.880 --> 48:57.080
He has a place
a couple of miles away.

48:57.160 --> 49:00.720
I have some business
to settle first with his accountant,

49:00.800 --> 49:02.880
so, you go on ahead with Kimber.

49:03.920 --> 49:05.480
Just me and him?

49:05.560 --> 49:07.800
Yeah. Till I'm done here.

49:08.680 --> 49:10.200
Is that all right?

49:12.200 --> 49:15.680
Look, I'll throw in an extra three quid
for your extra time.

49:17.960 --> 49:19.520
You think I'm a whore?

49:21.520 --> 49:24.120
Everyone's a whore, Grace.

49:24.200 --> 49:26.640
We just sell different parts
of ourselves.

49:26.720 --> 49:30.800
You said you wanted to work for me.
To do that you have to sharpen up.

49:30.880 --> 49:33.880
The deal is I give him
two hours with you.

49:33.960 --> 49:37.320
He thinks he's a ladies' man.
He thinks he can seduce you.

49:37.400 --> 49:40.080
Whenever you want,
just kick him in the balls.

49:40.160 --> 49:41.800
I'm a clause in a contract.

49:41.880 --> 49:46.360
If you want to be part of my organization,
you have to make sacrifices.

49:49.400 --> 49:50.640
Do we have a deal?

49:50.720 --> 49:53.480
As a sweetener
you can try your luck with mine.

49:54.400 --> 49:55.760
Yeah, we have a deal.

49:55.840 --> 49:57.680
- Two hours?
- Yeah.

49:57.840 --> 50:00.800
Side bet. Ten pounds says
I have her fucked in one.

50:02.960 --> 50:04.240
Mademoiselle.

50:14.760 --> 50:17.280
I bet he said you could
have me, didn't he?

50:18.920 --> 50:20.920
While he has her.

50:21.000 --> 50:23.240
That's the arrangement, isn't it?

50:26.440 --> 50:29.120
Well, yours might be
a prostitute but I'm not.

50:30.040 --> 50:32.000
I was a milliner when I met him.

50:33.120 --> 50:34.840
I was independent.

50:37.640 --> 50:39.160
I made this hat.

50:40.440 --> 50:41.960
I was a good milliner.

50:43.200 --> 50:45.120
It's a very pretty hat.

50:46.960 --> 50:48.360
Really is.

50:58.360 --> 51:00.160
So is she a prostitute?

51:01.880 --> 51:06.240
God's honest truth?
I don't know what she is.

51:15.120 --> 51:18.080
You showed me up
back there at the races.

51:21.040 --> 51:23.920
Why don't you teach me
how to dance properly?

51:25.080 --> 51:27.720
This music is too slow.
Put on a Charleston.

51:27.800 --> 51:31.040
Well, then we'd be far away.
I want to dance like this.

51:32.320 --> 51:35.280
Come on. You weren't so stiff
back there, were you?

51:37.160 --> 51:39.200
You ever been in a house this big?

51:40.440 --> 51:41.520
Hmm?

51:42.920 --> 51:44.120
Look at you.

51:47.000 --> 51:49.440
You look like a bloody film star.

51:54.080 --> 51:55.480
I want a cigarette.

52:06.400 --> 52:09.040
Oh, look, I've dropped something.
Pick it up.

52:09.120 --> 52:12.040
- Pick it up yourself.
- You're a fucking barmaid.

52:12.120 --> 52:14.880
If I drop a glass on the floor,
you pick it up.

52:14.960 --> 52:16.800
I want to watch you pick it up.

52:21.120 --> 52:23.920
Right, you little slag.
I have tried to be nice.

52:25.640 --> 52:29.040
If I drop a glass on the floor,

52:29.120 --> 52:34.480
you bend over and fucking pick up
the fucking glass. Okay?

52:34.560 --> 52:36.000
- What?
- No, sir, no.

52:36.080 --> 52:38.800
What are you doing here?
I've got another hour.

52:38.880 --> 52:42.640
Just wait. Listen to me.
Just listen to me.

52:42.720 --> 52:45.200
I was going to let you
go through with it,

52:45.280 --> 52:48.280
but in the end, my conscience
got the better of me.

52:48.360 --> 52:50.360
She looks good on the outside.

52:51.840 --> 52:53.560
But she has the clap.

52:56.160 --> 52:57.800
Yeah. Syphilis.

52:58.280 --> 53:01.280
When you took a shine to her,
I thought to use her.

53:01.360 --> 53:04.960
Somebody told me she had the syph,
I thought, "What the hell!"

53:06.520 --> 53:08.360
Call it my better nature.

53:09.360 --> 53:10.800
She's a whore.

53:16.200 --> 53:18.120
Now, just go
and wait in the car.

53:18.200 --> 53:19.840
I can walk on my own!

53:24.520 --> 53:27.360
Can we shake hands
and forget this ever happened?

53:51.720 --> 53:54.760
Start of the day
I was Lady Sarah of Connemara.

53:55.960 --> 53:59.000
By the end, I was a whore with the clap.

54:01.080 --> 54:03.880
You're a fucking bastard,
offering me like that.

54:09.520 --> 54:11.520
But then you changed your mind.

54:15.360 --> 54:17.520
Why did you change
your mind, Thomas?

54:22.560 --> 54:26.240
[theme song playing]
